1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,689
A stalk of yellow mustard flowers.

2
00:00:02,689 --> 00:00:04,085
A bunch of jasmine.

3
00:00:04,085 --> 00:00:06,577
A big sunflower, nodding in the sun.

4
00:00:06,977 --> 00:00:09,823
None of these is a
single flower on a stalk.

5
00:00:09,823 --> 00:00:12,894
Each one is many flowers,
arranged in a pattern.

6
00:00:13,344 --> 00:00:14,937
Diya noticed something odd.

7
00:00:14,937 --> 00:00:18,953
On the mustard stalk, the open flowers
were at the bottom, and the buds

8
00:00:18,953 --> 00:00:19,853
at the top.

9
00:00:19,802 --> 00:00:22,554
On the jasmine, the middle
flower opened first.

10
00:00:22,904 --> 00:00:26,717
That small clue tells us which of
two big patterns the plant follows.

11
00:00:27,217 --> 00:00:31,557
By the end, you will tell racemose
from cymose just by finding the oldest

12
00:00:31,557 --> 00:00:32,457
flower.

13
00:00:32,824 --> 00:00:34,502
First, what is a flower?

14
00:00:34,502 --> 00:00:36,324
A flower is a changed shoot.

15
00:00:36,774 --> 00:00:41,061
The tip of a shoot has a patch of
cells that keeps dividing, called the

16
00:00:41,061 --> 00:00:42,487
shoot apical meristem.

17
00:00:42,487 --> 00:00:45,796
To make a flower, it changes
into a floral meristem.

18
00:00:46,396 --> 00:00:49,670
Then the internodes stop growing
longer, and the axis becomes short and

19
00:00:49,670 --> 00:00:50,570
packed.

20
00:00:50,759 --> 00:00:55,225
The arrangement of flowers on the
floral axis is called inflorescence.

21
00:00:55,825 --> 00:00:57,434
There are two big types.

22
00:00:57,434 --> 00:01:01,840
The deciding question: does the tip
of the main axis keep growing, or does

23
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:03,357
it turn into a flower?

24
00:01:04,007 --> 00:01:05,384
Type one: racemose.

25
00:01:05,384 --> 00:01:06,770
Watch the main axis.

26
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:09,616
Here, the main axis keeps on growing.

27
00:01:09,616 --> 00:01:11,874
Its tip never turns into a flower.

28
00:01:12,624 --> 00:01:16,876
As it grows, it makes new flowers on
its sides, one after another, going

29
00:01:16,876 --> 00:01:17,776
up.

30
00:01:18,034 --> 00:01:22,237
So the oldest flowers are at the
bottom, and the youngest buds are at the

31
00:01:22,237 --> 00:01:23,137
top.

32
00:01:22,693 --> 00:01:25,582
This order is called
acropetal: towards the tip.

33
00:01:26,332 --> 00:01:28,129
Mustard shows this well.

34
00:01:28,129 --> 00:01:31,573
Its open flowers sit
below, and its buds above.

35
00:01:32,223 --> 00:01:33,393
Type two: cymose.

36
00:01:33,393 --> 00:01:34,878
Watch the tip this time.

37
00:01:35,228 --> 00:01:37,715
Here, the main axis ends in a flower.

38
00:01:37,715 --> 00:01:41,633
Once the tip becomes a flower,
the axis cannot grow any longer.

39
00:01:41,633 --> 00:01:43,109
Its growth is limited.

40
00:01:43,859 --> 00:01:48,280
New flowers then come from side
branches, below that first flower.

41
00:01:49,030 --> 00:01:53,404
So the oldest flower is at the top,
and the younger ones come below it.

42
00:01:53,404 --> 00:01:56,393
This order is called
basipetal: towards the base.

43
00:01:57,143 --> 00:01:58,681
Jasmine shows this well.

44
00:01:58,681 --> 00:02:02,377
The middle flower opens first,
then the flowers at its sides.

45
00:02:03,027 --> 00:02:05,101
Now, the two types side by side.

46
00:02:05,451 --> 00:02:08,524
Both carry many flowers
on one floral axis.

47
00:02:08,974 --> 00:02:13,443
But in racemose, the axis keeps
growing, and the oldest flower is at the

48
00:02:13,443 --> 00:02:14,343
base.

49
00:02:13,977 --> 00:02:18,846
In cymose, the axis ends in a flower,
and the oldest flower is at the top.

50
00:02:19,496 --> 00:02:20,396
Your turn.

51
00:02:20,351 --> 00:02:25,116
On a flower stalk, the biggest open
flower sits at the very tip, and small

52
00:02:25,116 --> 00:02:26,482
buds are below it.

53
00:02:26,482 --> 00:02:27,986
Racemose, or cymose?

54
00:02:27,986 --> 00:02:29,244
Pause and decide.

55
00:02:32,444 --> 00:02:33,344
Cymose!

56
00:02:33,076 --> 00:02:36,583
The tip became the first
flower, so it is the oldest.

57
00:02:36,583 --> 00:02:38,652
The younger buds come below it.

58
00:02:39,302 --> 00:02:40,302
Remember this.

59
00:02:40,302 --> 00:02:44,087
Racemose: the tip keeps growing,
oldest flowers at the base.

60
00:02:44,087 --> 00:02:47,498
Cymose: the tip becomes a
flower, oldest at the top.

61
00:02:48,098 --> 00:02:49,716
Now, a worked example.

62
00:02:49,716 --> 00:02:52,331
Let's sort four clues into the two types.

63
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:56,034
Open flowers at the
bottom, buds at the top.

64
00:02:56,034 --> 00:02:57,424
That is racemose.

65
00:02:57,874 --> 00:03:00,130
The main axis ends in a flower.

66
00:03:00,130 --> 00:03:01,330
That is cymose.

67
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:04,452
Flowers are made one
by one towards the tip.

68
00:03:04,452 --> 00:03:06,418
That is acropetal, so racemose.

69
00:03:06,868 --> 00:03:10,300
The middle flower opened
first, the side ones later.

70
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:11,460
That is cymose.

71
00:03:12,110 --> 00:03:13,575
Now, the exam trap.

72
00:03:13,925 --> 00:03:18,129
Many students think acropetal means the
oldest flower is at the tip, because

73
00:03:18,129 --> 00:03:19,178
acro means tip.

74
00:03:19,578 --> 00:03:22,814
But acropetal means the flowers
are made towards the tip.

75
00:03:22,814 --> 00:03:26,338
So the youngest is at the tip,
and the oldest at the base.

76
00:03:26,988 --> 00:03:28,430
Where is this asked?

77
00:03:28,430 --> 00:03:32,968
As statements about the main axis and
the order of flowers, or as labelled

78
00:03:32,968 --> 00:03:34,758
diagrams of the two types.

79
00:03:35,358 --> 00:03:36,537
Exam level now.

80
00:03:36,537 --> 00:03:39,851
Which statements are true
for racemose inflorescence?

81
00:03:39,851 --> 00:03:41,079
Pause and solve.

82
00:03:46,479 --> 00:03:47,743
The answer is B.

83
00:03:47,743 --> 00:03:50,152
Statements two and three are correct.

84
00:03:50,652 --> 00:03:55,311
In racemose, the main axis continues
to grow, and flowers are borne on

85
00:03:55,311 --> 00:03:57,368
the sides in acropetal order.

86
00:03:57,868 --> 00:04:01,833
An axis ending in a flower, with
basipetal order, describes cymose.

87
00:04:02,433 --> 00:04:03,973
Here is your recap.

88
00:04:03,973 --> 00:04:05,555
Screenshot this card.

89
00:04:08,755 --> 00:04:10,941
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

90
00:04:11,341 --> 00:04:12,241
One.

91
00:04:11,768 --> 00:04:14,578
In which type does the
main axis end in a flower?

92
00:04:15,478 --> 00:04:16,378
Two.

93
00:04:15,939 --> 00:04:19,142
Where is the oldest flower
in a racemose inflorescence?

94
00:04:20,042 --> 00:04:20,942
Three.

95
00:04:20,580 --> 00:04:22,432
What does basipetal order mean?

96
00:04:23,532 --> 00:04:27,944
Next time: cut a pea flower down the
middle, and only one cut gives matching

97
00:04:27,944 --> 00:04:28,844
halves.

98
00:04:28,532 --> 00:04:29,432
Why?

99
00:04:28,987 --> 00:04:30,689
We find out in the flower.
